.TH RODAUTH-ADMIN 8 "2026-09-07" "rodauth-admin 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
rodauth-admin \- operator CLI for the Rodauth Admin service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rodauth-admin install-gems
.br
.B rodauth-admin seal
.I NAME
.br
.B rodauth-admin check
.br
.B rodauth-admin migrate
.br
.B rodauth-admin rake \-\-
.I TASK
.RI [ ARG ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs as root.
Commissions and operates
.BR rodauth-admin.service ,
a single standalone Puma process serving the admin console on
.B 127.0.0.1:9292
and reached over an SSH tunnel:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ssh \-L 9292:127.0.0.1:9292 \fIhost\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
then
.IR http://localhost:9292 .
.PP
Everything that needs the application's secrets runs in a transient unit
.RB ( systemd-run (1)),
not in the calling shell, so the credential plumbing is exactly the one
the packaged unit uses: systemd decrypts as root, mounts the values 0400
under
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ,
and drops to the
.B rodauth-admin
uid before exec.
No secret passes through this program's environment or argv.
.SS install-gems
Installs the gems named by the shipped
.I Gemfile.lock
into the system Ruby's gem directory
.RI ( /var/lib/gems/3.3.0 ),
as root.
Refuses with exit 69 if
.BR gcc ,
.B make
or the Ruby headers are missing \(em
.BR argon2 ,
.BR puma ,
.BR nio4r ,
.BR bcrypt ,
.B json
and
.B bigdecimal
are compiled on the host.
Run by
.B postinst
on install and upgrade, and re-runnable by hand after fixing a failed
build.
.SS seal NAME
Seals one of the six secrets into the encrypted credential store.
.I NAME
must be one of the six listed under
.B ENVIRONMENT
below; anything else is exit 64.
The value is read from standard input when that is not a terminal, and
otherwise prompted for with
.RB ( read " " \-rs )
and never echoed.
It is written with
.B "printf '%s'"
into
.BR "systemd-creds encrypt \-\-with\-key=host \-\-name=" NAME,
under
.BR "umask 077" ,
producing
.IR /etc/credstore.encrypted/rodauth\-admin. NAME .cred .
Sealing again replaces the file; restart the service afterwards.
.SS check
Preflight.
Echoes each probe as
.RB \(lq "+ " command \(rq
and prints one
.RB \(lq "ok: " ... \(rq
or
.RB \(lq "FAIL: " ... \(rq
line per probe, exiting non-zero on the first
.BR FAIL .
In order: Ruby is 3.3.x; the
.B rodauth-admin
user exists;
.I /var/lib/rodauth-admin
exists;
.B "bundle check \-\-dry\-run"
passes
.I as the service user;
each of the six credential files is present and decryptable on this
host; at least one drop-in exists under
.IR /etc/systemd/system/rodauth\-admin.service.d ;
.B systemd-analyze verify
accepts the unit; and finally a transient unit carrying all six sealed
credentials runs
.B RodauthAdmin.boot!
so the application's own schema and secret checks run before
.B systemctl start
does.
Exit 0 means the service is ready to start.
.SS migrate
Prompts for
.B ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_MIGRATIONS
(or reads it from standard input), writes it 0600 root to
.I /run/rodauth-admin/migrations-url
on the tmpfs, and runs
.B rake db:migrate
in a transient unit with
.BI LoadCredential=ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_MIGRATIONS: that\ file
\(em systemd reads the source as root before dropping to the uid, so the
file need not be sealed.
A
.B trap
removes it on every exit path.
.B SetCredential=
is deliberately not used: it puts the value in the unit's properties, and
so on the D-Bus wire and in
.BR "systemctl show" .
The migration credential is never sealed onto the host and the running
service never holds it.
.SS rake \-\- TASK [ARG...]
Runs a rake task in a transient unit carrying the six sealed credentials:
.BR operators:add[ EMAIL ]\ REASON=... ,
.BR operators:list ,
.BR audit:recent ,
.BR authdb:status .
.B USER
is passed as
.BR ${SUDO_USER:-$USER} ,
which is what the Rakefile records as the audit actor.
The
.B \-\-
is required, so a task with switches survives argument parsing intact.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/rodauth-admin.service
The service unit. Never edit it; use a drop-in.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/rodauth-admin.service.d/10-authdb.conf
Per-host drop-in, written by the operator. The unit sets
.B IPAddressDeny=any
with only
.B localhost
allowed, so the authdb address must be allowed here:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Service]
IPAddressAllow=\fI10.0.0.5\fR/32
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IR /etc/credstore.encrypted/rodauth\-admin. NAME .cred
The six sealed secrets, 0600 root, in a directory shared with other
packages (0700 root, created by this package's tmpfiles.d fragment,
removed by none).
.TP
.I /usr/share/rodauth-admin
The application: an allowlist of
.IR config.ru ", " config/ ", " lib/ ", " views/ ", " db/ ", " Gemfile ", " Gemfile.lock ", " Rakefile
and a generated
.IR .bundle/config .
Read-only to the service.
.TP
.I /var/lib/rodauth-admin
State directory, 0750, and
.B HOME
for the service and its transient units. Bundler wants a writable home;
the application writes nothing here.
.TP
.I /run/rodauth-admin/migrations-url
Exists only for the duration of one
.BR migrate .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/rodauth-admin/with-credentials
The exec wrapper that turns
.B $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY
files into environment variables.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The six variables the service holds, each from a sealed credential of the
same name:
.TP
.B RODAUTH_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET
Rack session secret.
.TP
.B AUTH_SECRET
Rodauth's HMAC secret.
.TP
.B ARGON2_SECRET
Argon2 pepper for password hashing.
.TP
.B ADMIN_DATABASE_URL
Read/write connection for the admin's own tables.
.TP
.B ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_RO
Read-only connection used for lookups.
.TP
.B ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_VERBS
Connection for the verb-code role.
.PP
Set by the unit, override with a drop-in:
.TP
.B PORT
Default
.BR 9292 .
.TP
.B RODAUTH_ADMIN_BIND
Default
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
Changing it does not open the service up:
.B IPAddressDeny=any
in the unit is the real boundary.
.TP
.B RODAUTH_ADMIN_HOST
Default
.BR localhost ,
the hostname the browser sees through the tunnel.
.PP
Also set by the unit:
.B RACK_ENV=production
and
.BR HOME=/var/lib/rodauth-admin .
.B ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_MIGRATIONS
is used only by
.BR migrate .
.SH NOTES
Values are sealed with
.B "printf '%s'"
and read back with
.BR "$(cat ...)" ,
so trailing newlines are stripped in both directions and the value the
application sees is byte-for-byte what was typed.
Piping a value with
.BR echo (1)
therefore also works, but
.B printf
is what the CLI does and what the runbook shows.
.PP
.B \-\-with\-key=host
means the sealed files are decryptable only on the host that created
them: re-imaging the VM, or copying
.I /etc/credstore.encrypted
to another machine, invalidates every
.I .cred
file. Re-seal all six after a rebuild.
.B auto
is deliberately not used \(em it would silently pick TPM2 on a future
host and make the same files undecryptable in a less obvious way.
.PP
The secrets end up in Puma's process environment, so
.I /proc/<pid>/environ
holds them, readable by the service user and root only;
.B ProtectProc=invisible
hides the process from everyone else.
That is the price of keeping the application env-var-only, and is still
better than
.BR EnvironmentFile= :
nothing is in cleartext on disk and
.B systemctl show
reveals nothing.
.PP
The database URLs must use IP literals.
.B IPAddressDeny=any
blocks DNS along with everything else, so a hostname in a URL fails to
resolve at connect time \(em unless the drop-in also allows the
resolver's address.
.PP
Gems installed under
.I /var/lib/gems
are not removed on purge; they belong to the system Ruby and may be
shared. Remove them with
.BR "gem uninstall" (1)
if they are not wanted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
.RB ( EX_USAGE )
Bad invocation: unknown subcommand, unknown credential name, missing
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B 69
.RB ( EX_UNAVAILABLE )
A prerequisite is missing: not root, no toolchain or Ruby headers, no
.BR systemd-creds ,
no credential store directory.
.TP
.B 78
.RB ( EX_CONFIG )
A configuration problem: an empty value refused by
.BR seal ,
or the first failing probe in
.BR check .
The unit's
.B RestartPreventExitStatus=78
mirrors this: the application exits 78 on a
.B ConfigurationError
and systemd does not retry.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-creds (1),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR systemd.resource-control (5),
.BR sysusers.d (5),
.BR tmpfiles.d (5),
.BR deb-systemd-helper (1p),
.BR ssh (1)
